Z||000000||MooCow||04-25-2001||01:11 PM||||Well I think I am going to change the head gasket and see ifthat helps out any.  I went to drive the car today to see if it still smoked and when I started it up it just died.  So I tried it again and this time with some gas and it studered a little bit and poof white smoke came out the exhaust.  I went for a drive and no smoke would conme out.  Even after I nailed the gas like before nothing.  I also don't have my top end speed all there.  So I need to know how to change the head gaskets.<P>                              Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||Kerri 98||04-25-2001||02:09 PM||||I guess I missed all your car symptoms somewhere in the board.  What makes you think its the head gasket? Oil in cooling fluid? Cooling fluid in Oil? Is it blowing a kind of smoke "like in the morning when its cold."? Does the oil cap have a chocolate milk appearance? Does it shoot out coolant from the radiator if the cap is off and the engine is accelerated??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||04-25-2001||02:44 PM||||When you start assuming things, all you can assume is you are probably wrong. If you have no oil in your water, no water in your oil,(Looks like gravy) and are not loosing coolant, you don't have a blown head gasket.<BR>a bit of oil water mix at the oil fill means nothing, condensation can do that,look at your dip stick.<BR> You can't diagnose by throwing money at a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-25-2001||08:52 PM||||MooCow,<P>No oil in water and no water in oil, correct?<P>Pull your spark plugs and do a compression check.<BR>Post your results, it'll be a dead give away if you have a bad head gasket (or two). ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||04-25-2001||10:04 PM||||Replacing your head gasket is going to be a very big job, and you could be out of your car for a long time if youre new at doing it.  It involves taking off the rocker covers, intake and exhaust manifolds, removing the push rods after you have loosened the rockers and turned them out of the way, and then trying to get off the heads themselves.  Once you've gotten that far, you will have to put some rags in the cylenders and then very carefully scrape off your old gasket from both the block and the head, and be very careful not to make a mark on any of the sealing surfaces.  And then after that, you'll have to reassemble all of it, and torque every bolt that you took off back to spec.<P>Just as a note, I have never done one, but these are the steps my hayne's manual says for the 2.5  I bet it's probably a general list of procedures, even for a 3.4<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
